{:edb/db-before-t 1351 :edb/db-after-t 1352 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534664 50 #inst "2026-10-12T06:35:28.603Z" 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186126773 1075 #edb/ref 17592186126774 13194139534664 17592186045417 false] [17592186126773 1075 #edb/ref 17592186126775 13194139534664 17592186045417 false] [17592186126773 1075 #edb/ref 17592186126776 13194139534664 17592186045417 false] [17592186126773 1075 #edb/ref 17592186126777 13194139534664 17592186045417 false] [17592186126773 1075 #edb/ref 17592186126778 13194139534664 17592186045417 false] [17592186126773 1075 #edb/ref 17592186431715 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186126773 1075 #edb/ref 17592186431724 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186126773 1075 #edb/ref 17592186431732 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186126773 1075 #edb/ref 17592186431733 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186126773 1075 #edb/ref 17592186431741 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186126773 1078 #edb/ref 17592186431724 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186126797 1075 #edb/ref 17592186126798 13194139534664 17592186045417 false] [17592186126797 1075 #edb/ref 17592186126799 13194139534664 17592186045417 false] [17592186126797 1075 #edb/ref 17592186126800 13194139534664 17592186045417 false] [17592186126797 1075 #edb/ref 17592186126801 13194139534664 17592186045417 false] [17592186126797 1075 #edb/ref 17592186126802 13194139534664 17592186045417 false] [17592186126797 1075 #edb/ref 17592186431716 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186126797 1075 #edb/ref 17592186431718 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186126797 1075 #edb/ref 17592186431719 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186126797 1075 #edb/ref 17592186431735 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186126797 1075 #edb/ref 17592186431736 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186126797 1078 #edb/ref 17592186431735 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186129273 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431765 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186129274 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431762 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186129274 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431764 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186129289 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431761 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186129290 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431763 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186129290 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431766 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186129370 1050 1440.0 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186129546 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186129546 1071 "Consider a plane lamina of mass $3\\,\\text{kg}$ and center of mass $(1,1),$ where $x$ and $y$ are measured in meters. If the moment of inertia of the lamina about an axis perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass is $1.5\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2},$ what is the moment of inertia of the lamina about an axis parallel to the first passing through the point $(-3,4)?$" 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186129546 1071 "Consider a plane lamina of mass $3kg$ and center of mass $(1, 1)$, where $x$ and $y$ are measured in meters. If the moment of inertia of the lamina about an axis perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass is $1.5kg \\cdot m^{2}$, what is the moment of inertia of the lamina about an axis parallel to the first passing through the point $(-3, 4)$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139534664 17592186045417 false] [17592186129546 1074 "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line ${l}_{M}$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to ${l}_{M}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and ${I}_{M}$ and ${I}_{l}$ are the moments of inertia about the lines ${l}_{M}$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nThe distance between the line through the center of mass $(1,1)$ and the line through the point $(-3,4)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{(-3-1)^{2}+(4-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, applying the parallel axis theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{l} & =1.5+{5}^{2}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =1.5+75 \\\\  & =76.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia is $76.5\\,kg\\cdot {m}^{2}.$" 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186129550 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186129550 1071 "A circular plate of mass $6\\,\\text{kg}$ with uniform mass density occupies the region of a disk $D$ of radius $3\\,\\text{m}$ and is centered at the origin.\nGiven that the moment of inertia of the plate about a line $l$ perpendicular to the plate is $39\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2},$ find the distance of the line $l$ from the center of mass of $D.$" 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186129550 1071 "A circular plate of mass $6kg$ with uniform mass density occupies the region of a disk $D$ of radius $3m$ and is centered at the origin.\nGiven that the moment of inertia of the plate about a line $l$ perpendicular to the plate is $39kg \\cdot m^{2}$, find the distance of the line $l$ from the center of mass of $D$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139534664 17592186045417 false] [17592186129550 1074 "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line ${l}_{M}$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to ${l}_{M}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and ${I}_{M}$ and ${I}_{l}$ are the moments of inertia about the lines ${l}_{M}$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ can be expressed in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe area of the circular plate $D$ is $A=\\pi \\cdot {3}^{2}=9\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{2}.$\nSince the plate has a uniform density, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda (r,\\theta )=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{6}{9\\pi }=\\frac{2}{3\\pi }\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2},\n$$\n\nand its center of mass is its centroid $(0,0).$\n\nThen, the moment of inertia about the perpendicular line through the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{M} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}{|}_{0}^{3}\\cdot \\theta {|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{81}{4}\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\  & =27\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the parallel axis theorem and solving for $d,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M \\\\ 39 & =27+6{d}^{2} \\\\ 6{d}^{2} & =12 \\\\ {d}^{2} & =2 \\\\ d & =\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186130459 1064 "Consider a plane lamina that occupies the region $D \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2$ of mass $M$ where the center of mass is at $\\left(\\bar{x},\\bar{y}\\right).$\n\n![](images/4c/4c2c7e3525dfe8aea3cce69a7332ccf5ca82ddfe37657071ddef35650bcea383.png)\n\nWe know that the lamina has some moment of inertia $I_M$ about the line $l_M$ that passes through the center of mass and is perpendicular to the lamina.\n\n![](images/96/9638e9d47ef3c9b2fab47f39cf70e2ba07863c6e949755c8378e5737caa0a350.png)\n\nNow, if $l$ is a line parallel to $l_M,$ then the lamina also has moment of inertia about $l,$ say $I_l.$\n\n![](images/06/06819ca45bd176ff9d36b07c73574bc9f2911a7b0b96b5b916ba76f8606d2f60.png)\n\nGiven the value of $I_M,$ we can find $I_l$ using the **parallel axis theorem**, which states that\n\n$$\nI_l = I_M + d^2M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines.\n\nThe term $d^2M$ is the moment of inertia about $l$ of the lamina when its entire mass is concentrated at the center of mass.\n\n![](images/fb/fbab9fab43dd45fd80008704f406f94864cac57d636320d250dca86ff465f196.png)\n\nSo, our interpretation of the parallel axis theorem is the following:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\text{moment of inertia of }D \\ \\text{about a parallel line }l}_{I_l}\n\\:=\\:\n\\underbrace{\\text{moment of inertia of }D \\ \\text{about the center of mass}}_{I_M}\n\\:+\\:\n\\underbrace{\\text{moment of inertia of point mass }M \\ \\text{about the parallel line } l}_{d^2M}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $d^2M$ is always positive, we have $I_M \\leq I_l$ for any line $l$ perpendicular to the lamina. In other words, the moment of inertia about the line through its center of mass is the *smallest* moment of inertia about any line in that direction." 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186130459 1064 "Consider a plane lamina that occupies the region $D \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2$ of mass $M$ where the center of mass is at $\\left(\\bar{x},\\bar{y}\\right).$\n\n![](images/4c/4c2c7e3525dfe8aea3cce69a7332ccf5ca82ddfe37657071ddef35650bcea383.png)\n\nWe know that the lamina has some moment of inertia $I_M$ about the line $l_M$ that passes through the center of mass and is perpendicular to the lamina.\n\n![](images/96/9638e9d47ef3c9b2fab47f39cf70e2ba07863c6e949755c8378e5737caa0a350.png)\n\nNow, if $l$ is a line parallel to $l_M,$ then the lamina also has moment of inertia about $l,$ say $I_l.$\n\n![](images/06/06819ca45bd176ff9d36b07c73574bc9f2911a7b0b96b5b916ba76f8606d2f60.png)\n\nGiven the value of $I_M,$ we can find $I_l$ using the **parallel axis theorem**, which states that\n\n$$\nI_l = I_M + d^2M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines.\n\nTo demonstrate, suppose the lamina above has mass [math]4\\,\\rm{kg}[/math] and the moment of inertia about the line [math]l_M[/math] perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass is [math]6.4\\rm{kg}\\cdot\\rm{m}^2.[/math]\n\nIf the other line [math]l[/math] parallel to [math]l_M[/math] is at a distance of [math]d=2\\,\\rm{m}[/math] from it, then, by the parallel axis theorem, the moment of inertia about [math]l[/math] is\n\n\\begin{align*}\nI_l\t&= I_M + d^2M \\\\[3pt]\n\t&= 6.4 + 2^2 \\cdot 4 \\\\[3pt]\n\t&= 6.4 + 16 \\\\[3pt]\n\t&= 22.4 \\,\\rm{kg} \\cdot \\rm{m}^2.\n\\end{align*}\n\nThe term $d^2M$ is the moment of inertia about $l$ of the lamina when its entire mass is concentrated at the center of mass.\n\n![](images/fb/fbab9fab43dd45fd80008704f406f94864cac57d636320d250dca86ff465f196.png)\n\nSo, our interpretation of the parallel axis theorem is the following:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\text{moment of inertia of }D \\ \\text{about a parallel line }l}_{I_l}\n\\:=\\:\n\\underbrace{\\text{moment of inertia of }D \\ \\text{about the center of mass}}_{I_M}\n\\:+\\:\n\\underbrace{\\text{moment of inertia of point mass }M \\ \\text{about the parallel line } l}_{d^2M}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $d^2M$ is always positive, we have $I_M \\leq I_l$ for any line $l$ perpendicular to the lamina. In other words, the moment of inertia about the line through its center of mass is the *smallest* moment of inertia about any line in that direction." 13194139534664 17592186045417 false] [17592186431715 1080 "72.5\\,kg\\cdot {m}^{2}" 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186431715 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186431715 1083 #uuid "0878c284-4879-55be-912a-9656fbbc7c94" 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186431716 1080 "\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{m}" 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186431716 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186431716 1083 #uuid "0993c715-5235-5e81-99f8-e0df49df41fd" 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186431717 1080 "\\frac{2\\pi }{3e}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186431717 1082 #edb/ref 1759218