39534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186431760 1075 #edb/ref 17592186431726 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186431760 1075 #edb/ref 17592186431729 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186431760 1075 #edb/ref 17592186431738 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186431760 1075 #edb/ref 17592186431742 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186431760 1075 #edb/ref 17592186431744 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186431760 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186431760 1077 "selection" 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186431760 1078 #edb/ref 17592186431729 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186431760 1079 #uuid "ef625286-2a13-50df-81d4-0c23373fdb51" 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186431761 1068 #edb/ref 17592186431757 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186431761 1069 "q-164146" 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186431761 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186431761 1071 "A rectangular plate occupies the region\n$D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 6\\}$\nand has mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{1}{12}y,$ where $x$ and $y$ are measured in meters. The moment of inertia of the plate about a line $l$ perpendicular to the plate is $\\frac{31}{4}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.$\nGiven that the center of mass of the plate is at $(1,4),$ find the distance of the line $l$ from the center of mass." 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186431761 1072 #uuid "0d33e1be-cff3-50e5-94dd-6992342f9938" 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186431761 1074 "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line ${l}_{M}$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to ${l}_{M}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and ${I}_{M}$ and ${I}_{l}$ are the moments of inertia about the lines ${l}_{M}$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nFirst, we find the mass of the plate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{1}{12}y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{6}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot x\\,{|}_{0}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot 2\\cdot 18 \\\\  & =3\\,\\text{kg}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the moment of inertia about the perpendicular line through the center of mass $(1,4)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{M} & ={∬}_{D}((x-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}+(y-\\overset{―}{y})^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}((x-1)^{2}+(y-4)^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{6}((x-1)^{2}+(y-4)^{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{12}y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{6}(x-1)^{2}y+{y}^{3}-8{y}^{2}+16y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)^{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{4}-\\frac{8}{3}{y}^{3}+8{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=6}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}{\\int }_{0}^{2}18(x-1)^{2}+324-576+288\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}{\\int }_{0}^{2}18{x}^{2}-36x+54\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}{[6{x}^{3}-18{x}^{2}+54x]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(48-72+108) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot 84 \\\\  & =7\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the parallel axis theorem and solving for $d,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M \\\\ \\frac{31}{4} & =7+3{d}^{2} \\\\ 3{d}^{2} & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ {d}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ d & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186431762 1068 #edb/ref 17592186431759 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186431762 1069 "q-163814" 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186431762 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186431762 1071 "A circular plate of mass $4\\,\\text{kg}$ and uniform mass density occupies the region of a disk $D$ of radius $1\\,\\text{m}$ centered at the origin.\nFind the moment of inertia of the plate about a line perpendicular to $D$ at a distance of $2\\,\\text{m}$ from a parallel line passing through its center of mass." 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186431762 1072 #uuid "7f698f1e-e965-5c52-9d4f-e9787cc01568" 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186431762 1074 "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line ${l}_{M}$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to ${l}_{M}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and ${I}_{M}$ and ${I}_{l}$ are the moments of inertia about the lines ${l}_{M}$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ can be expressed in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe area of the circular plate $D$ is $A=\\pi \\cdot {1}^{2}=\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{2}.$\nSince the plate has a uniform density, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda (r,\\theta )=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2},\n$$\n\nand its center of mass is its centroid $(0,0).$\n\nThen, the moment of inertia about the perpendicular line through the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{M} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}{|}_{0}^{1}\\cdot \\theta {|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\  & =2\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the parallel axis theorem, the moment of inertia about a line $2\\,\\text{m}$ from the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M \\\\  & =2+{2}^{2}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =2+16 \\\\  & =18\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186431763 1068 #edb/ref 17592186431756 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186431763 1069 "q-164148" 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186431763 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186431763 1071 "A rectangular plate occupies the region\n$D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 6\\}$\nand has mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{1}{6}x,$ where $x$ and $y$ are measured in meters.\nGiven that the center of mass is at $(2,3),$ find the moment of inertia about a line perpendicular to $D$ at a distance of $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,\\text{m}$ from a parallel line passing through its center of mass." 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186431763 1072 #uuid "883c2bc8-ea50-58fe-851b-c16e2097cdb5" 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186431763 1074 "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line ${l}_{M}$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to ${l}_{M}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and ${I}_{M}$ and ${I}_{l}$ are the moments of inertia about the lines ${l}_{M}$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nFirst, we find the mass of the plate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{1}{6}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{3}x\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}{|}_{0}^{3}\\cdot y{|}_{0}^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the moment of inertia about the perpendicular line through the center of mass $(2,3)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{M} & ={∬}_{D}((x-\\overset{¯}{x})^{2}+(y-\\overset{¯}{y})^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}((x-2)^{2}+(y-3)^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{6}((x-2)^{2}+(y-3)^{2})\\frac{x}{6}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{6}x(x-2)^{2}+x(y-3)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{[x(x-2)^{2}y+\\frac{1}{3}x(y-3)^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=6}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{3}6x(x-2)^{2}+18x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{3}6{x}^{3}-24{x}^{2}+42x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{[\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{4}-8{x}^{3}+21{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(\\frac{243}{2}-216+189-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\frac{189}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{63}{4}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the parallel axis theorem, the moment of inertia about a line $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,\\text{m}$ from the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M \\\\  & =\\frac{63}{4}+{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{63}{4}+\\frac{9}{4} \\\\  & =18\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186431764 1068 #edb/ref 17592186431758 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186431764 1069 "q-163817" 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186431764 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186431764 1071 "Calculate the moment of inertia of a circular plate that occupies the region of a disk $D$ of radius $1\\,\\text{m}$ with mass density $\\lambda (x,y)={e}^{-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}},$ measured in $\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2},$ about a line perpendicular to the disk passing through a point on the circumference." 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186431764 1072 #uuid "9b0cf2d4-19bf-5bdf-8414-97d0c52886bd" 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186431764 1074 "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line ${l}_{M}$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to ${l}_{M}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and ${I}_{M}$ and ${I}_{l}$ are the moments of inertia about the lines ${l}_{M}$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ can be expressed in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nSince the mass density is symmetric in $x$ and $y,$ its center of mass is at $(0,0).$ The mass density function, in polar coordinates, is given by $\\lambda (r,\\theta )={e}^{-{r}^{2}}.$\n\nNext, we find the mass of the plate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{-{r}^{2}}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}r{e}^{-{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-{r}^{2}}{|}_{0}^{1}\\cdot \\theta {|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\frac{1}{e})\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\pi (1-\\frac{1}{e})\\,\\text{kg}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the moment of inertia about the perpendicular line through the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{M} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{-{r}^{2}}\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{3}{e}^{-{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}({r}^{2}+1){e}^{-{r}^{2}}{|}_{0}^{1}\\cdot \\theta {|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{e})\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\pi (1-\\frac{2}{e})\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the parallel axis theorem, the moment of inertia about a line passing through a point on the circumference is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M \\\\  & =\\pi (1-\\frac{2}{e})+{1}^{2}\\cdot \\pi (1-\\frac{1}{e}) \\\\  & =\\pi (1-\\frac{2}{e}+1-\\frac{1}{e}) \\\\  & =\\pi (2-\\frac{3}{e})\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186431765 1068 #edb/ref 17592186431755 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186431765 1069 "q-163823" 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186431765 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186431765 1071 "Consider a plane lamina of mass $5\\,\\text{kg}.$ If the moment of inertia of the lamina about an axis perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass is $1.5\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2},$ what is the moment of inertia of the lamina about an axis parallel to the first passing through a point on the lamina at a distance of $20\\,\\text{cm}$ from its center of mass?" 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186431765 1072 #uuid "9cecef3e-71f6-5736-b423-67aa582494ea" 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186431765 1074 "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line ${l}_{M}$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to ${l}_{M}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and ${I}_{M}$ and ${I}_{l}$ are the moments of inertia about the lines ${l}_{M}$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nThe distance between the lines is $d=20\\,\\text{cm}=0.2\\,\\text{m}.$ Then, applying the parallel axis theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{l} & =1.5+(0.2)^{2}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =1.5+0.2 \\\\  & =1.7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia is $1.7\\,kg\\cdot {m}^{2}.$" 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186431766 1068 #edb/ref 17592186431760 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186431766 1069 "q-164143" 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186431766 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186431766 1071 "A rectangular plate of mass $\\frac{9}{2}\\,\\text{kg}$ and uniform mass density occupies the region\n$D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 4,\\,0\\le y\\le 4\\},$\nwhere $x$ and $y$ are measured in meters.\nFind the moment of inertia of the plate about a line perpendicular to $D$ at a distance of $2\\,\\text{m}$ from a parallel line passing through its center of mass." 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186431766 1072 #uuid "a98b9898-9990-5bdc-a565-d49f3ea3b73d" 13194139534664 17592186045417 true] [17592186431766 1074 "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line ${l}_{M}$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to ${l}_{M}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and ${I}_{M}$ and ${I}_{l}$ are the moments of inertia about the lines ${l}_{M}$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nThe area of the rectangular plate $D$ is $A=4\\cdot 4=16\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}.$\nSince the plate has a uniform density, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{(\\frac{9}{2})}{16}=\\frac{9}{32}\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2},\n$$\n\nand its center of mass is its centroid:\n\n$$\n(\\overset{¯}{x},\\overset{¯}{y})=(\\frac{0+4}{2},\\frac{0+4}{2})=(2,2)\n$$\n\nThen, the moment of inertia about the perpendicular line through the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{M} & ={∬}_{D}((x-\\overset{¯}{x})^{2}+(y-\\overset{¯}{y})^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{32}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{4}9(x-2)^{2}+9(y-2)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{32}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{[9(x-2)^{2}y+3(y-2)^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{32}{\\int }_{0}^{4}36(x-2)^{2}+48\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{32}{[12(x-2)^{3}+48x]}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{32}(96+192-(-96)) \\\\  & =12\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the parallel axis theorem, the moment of inertia about a line $2\\,\\text{m}$ from the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M \\\\  & =12+{2}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =12+18 \\\\  & =30\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534664 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-0878c284-4879-55be-912a-9656fbbc7c94" 17592186431715 "answer-0993c715-5235-5e81-99f8-e0df49df41fd" 17592186431716 "answer-0a863b7c-edfd-5ed3-b733-c20e15e56c23" 17592186431717 "answer-13c67ee2-4b4a-554c-addc-8d24167bac80" 17592186431718 "answer-217da9bf-7efe-5f53-aedd-b0bd54330075" 17592186431719 "answer-2f7be1d0-d901-57d1-9262-308cd4b16cb7" 17592186431720 "answer-3179da0e-3638-5470-946e-1ba68d8e7a41" 17592186431721 "answer-33730752-03eb-5584-b1de-cb94ac16c08b" 17592186431722 "answer-47e8cfaf-fd1b-55f7-8116-1f0008be7b3d" 17592186431723 "answer-4cdc4a8e-a57f-57e0-bb50-35bd39671509" 17592186431724 "answer-56e8f7cf-5f92-5e36-9bcd-9ee37ee18eb7" 17592186431725 "answer-59e32abc-46af-5bfb-8a64-d906d04f1614" 17592186431726 "answer-764d4bb7-3ff1-5f4b-acc9-78c80102bfee" 17592186431727 "answer-82451b16-4f7c-56b9-8377-2102d860c661" 17592186431728 "answer-82747faa-ed1e-5a88-b125-47a8d260239f" 17592186431729 "answer-85a7edbf-02fe-5b24-a716-394fd89efa4e" 17592186431730 "answer-874f21f4-6ea1-564f-aaae-2fe439042176" 17592186431731 "answer-8a45685d-72c3-5e27-a219-4717f3dcc1b8" 17592186431732 "answer-8a6a1caf-3346-52e6-8e61-695021764e2f" 17592186431733 "answer-8b2581c2-d3fa-5830-911b-7de2d4f92b75" 17592186431734 "answer-8e2eecdc-200a-5731-8433-4d7de3a0d3fb" 17592186431735 "answer-8ec20448-d6e9-5faa-8568-2a10c85abae3" 17592186431736 "answer-913539ca-2d7a-54d5-b8ee-0ae5cbab6a86" 17592186431737 "answer-95a1c2ed-0b14-59fe-afd7-b201b865e7d9" 17592186431738 "ans